{
  "dom_id": "step-q90962",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nI. $f:[0,\\infty )\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$\n\nII. $g:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $g(x)=|x-1|+1$\n\nIII. $h:(0,\\infty )\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)=\\sqrt{x}$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is an into function. Let's take $y=1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}=1.$ In fact, $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}\\ge 2$ for all $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- II is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$  such that $g(x)=|x-1|+1=0.$ In fact, $|x-1|+1\\ge 1$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ such that $h(x)=\\sqrt{x}=0.$ In fact, $\\sqrt{x}>0$ for all $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
